          "text": "The pattern indexes as a **primitive tetragonal** cell with approximately \\(a=6.254\\) \u00c5 and \\(c=5.055\\) \u00c5. The systematic intensity distribution is consistent with **P4/mmm (No. 123), Z = 1**, Pearson symbol **tP7**.\n\nRepresentative indexing is:\n\n| 2\u03b8 (\u00b0) | hkl |\n|---:|:---|\n| 14.15 | 100 |\n| 17.53 | 001 |\n| 20.06 | 110 |\n| 22.60 | 101 |\n| 26.76 | 111 |\n| 28.52 | 200 |\n| 31.97 | 210 |\n| 33.67 | 201 |\n| 35.49 | 002 |\n| 36.69 | 211 |\n| 38.38 | 102 |\n| 40.78 | 220 |\n\nThe weak 001 and 110 reflections arise from Ba/Mg/Ge scattering cancellation, while the strong even-index reflections support atoms on the \\(z=0\\) and \\(z=1/2\\) special planes. The Li/vacancy ordering is less strongly constrained by Cu X-rays, but the 100/101 and 102/211 intensity relationships favor Li on 1c and 1d, leaving 1b vacant.\n\n### Primary CIF\n\n```cif\ndata_BaLi2Mg2Ge2\n_audit_creation_method\n'Indexed and solved from the supplied Cu Kalpha1 powder pattern'\n\n_chemical_name_common                 'BaLi2(MgGe)2'\n_chemical_formula_sum                 'Ba Li2 Mg2 Ge2'\n_chemical_formula_structural          'BaLi2Mg2Ge2'\n_cell_formula_units_Z                 1\n\n_space_group_crystal_system           tetragonal\n_space_group_name_H-M_alt             'P 4/m m m'\n_space_group_IT_number                123\n_symmetry_space_group_name_H-M        'P 4/m m m'\n\n_cell_length_a                        6.2543\n_cell_length_b                        6.2543\n_cell_length_c                        5.0550\n_cell_angle_alpha                     90\n_cell_angle_beta                      90\n_cell_angle_gamma                     90\n_cell_volume                          197.73\n\n_exptl_crystal_density_diffrn         2.898\n_diffrn_radiation_type                'Cu Kalpha1'\n_diffrn_radiation_wavelength          1.5406\n\nloop_\n_space_group_symop_id\n_space_group_symop_operation_xyz\n1   'x,y,z'\n2   '-y,x,z'\n3   '-x,-y,z'\n4   'y,-x,z'\n5   '-x,y,z'\n6   'x,-y,z'\n7   'y,x,z'\n8   '-y,-x,z'\n9   'x,y,-z'\n10  '-y,x,-z'\n11  '-x,-y,-z'\n12  'y,-x,-z'\n13  '-x,y,-z'\n14  'x,-y,-z'\n15  'y,x,-z'\n16  '-y,-x,-z'\n\nloop_\n_atom_site_label\n_atom_site_type_symbol\n_atom_site_symmetry_multiplicity\n_atom_site_Wyckoff_symbol\n_atom_site_fract_x\n_atom_site_fract_y\n_atom_site_fract_z\n_atom_site_occupancy\nBa1  Ba  1  a  0.000000  0.000000  0.000000  1.0\nLi1  Li  1  c  0.500000  0.500000  0.000000  1.0\nLi2  Li  1  d  0.500000  0.500000  0.500000  1.0\nMg1  Mg  2  e  0.000000  0.500000  0.000000  1.0\nGe1  Ge  2  f  0.000000  0.500000  0.500000  1.0\n```\n\nNo distinct heavy-atom alternative is required. Because Li scatters weakly in Cu X-ray data, neutron diffraction would be the preferred independent confirmation of the Li/vacancy ordering.",
